###############################################################################
# Q10 -- Benchmark results
###############################################################################

# No energy/force/stress accuracy metric is reported in a form the schema captures (see Q12).

###############################################################################
# Q11 -- Computational resources
###############################################################################

# The paper reports no training-cost or inference-cost figures.

###############################################################################
# Q12 -- Gaps and ambiguities (prose; not triples)
###############################################################################

# - No AccuracyMetric: energy and force MAEs on the test sets against compute time per atom and MD step (Fig. 2), GPU speeds, learning curves, energy-volume curves and the extrapolation tests (Laves phases, silica under compression) are figures. The tabulated material (Tables 1 to 9) is a grading scheme and user-friendliness comments.
# - The paper fits several models per potential type with different sizes (basis functions, layers, features) and marks one per type for the later tests; one run per type and system is encoded with the shared cutoff only (5 A for Si-O, 7.6 A for Al-Cu-Zr; for message-passing models this is the cutoff of a single shell). For Al-Cu-Zr the GAPs use only 10% of the training data because of memory. Fitting codes are named in the run labels and not asserted as mlips:hasImplementation, since several are not vocabulary libraries.
# - Reference DFT settings and data-set sizes are in the cited earlier studies and the Zenodo record; the data-set nodes carry no reference calculation. Whether stresses were fitted varies by code (stress-enabled NequIP and Allegro variants are mentioned).
# - Read from the arXiv v1 preprint.
# - Author affiliations are not encoded (ORCIDs are those deposited with the DOI metadata, source: crossref).
